{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And all the substance that was at their feet.</b> Rashi explained: this is a man's wealth, which stands him on his feet. But is someone who has no wealth crippled in his feet — did all his wisdom and the rest of his human qualities not stand him on his feet? Rather, it appears that he is speaking in denigration of the acquisition of wealth, for it is the lowest of all four qualities enumerated by the Ramban (in Eight Chapters), and they are: wisdom, and strength, and character traits, and wealth — for all of them are attached to the person, while wealth is external to him. For wisdom has its source in the head and the brain and the heart, and strength has its source in the heart, for one who is faint of heart is no warrior; but wealth is attached to his foot, for ״יִתְרוֹן אֶרֶץ בַּכֹּל הִיא״ (Ecclesiastes 5:8) — and the earth is trampled under his feet. It is fitting, then, that it be the lowest of them all and be placed under the dominion of man, to do with his wealth whatever he wishes. But in the end the order is reversed: his wealth overpowers him, to whatever it desires it leads him, and it rises from the earth from its lowly station. Just as a man rises up against his fellow, so does his wealth rise up against him, ruling over him and carrying him against his own will and against the will of his owner. Therefore it called it ״הַיְקוּם אֲשֶׁר בְּרַגְלֵיהֶם״ — for this hurtful, despised thing, seized with cords of scorn and placed beneath the soles of his feet, is ״הַיְקוּם״ — that which rises up against him to rule over him. And just as ״כַּאֲשֶׁר יָקוּם אִישׁ עַל רֵעֵהוּ וּרְצָחוֹ נָפֶשׁ״ (Deuteronomy 22:26), so does this acquisition bring its owner into many dangers. And this is what killed Korah, for he trusted in his wealth and fell. Therefore he called the wealth of Korah and his assembly ״הַיְקוּם אֲשֶׁר בְּרַגְלֵיהֶם.״"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {0 "The text attributes the four qualities to הרמב״ם; in his Eight Chapters the Rambam does enumerate these four perfections, with wealth explicitly named as the lowest. The verse citation appears as (דברים כ״ג:כ״ו) in the source, but the phrase quoted matches Deuteronomy 22:26; this is likely a typographical error in the edition."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:12:12.463040298Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}